WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.490
A useful starting point is to separate trusted templates from untrusted
data.

2
00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:09.661
Templates written and reviewed by the application team are intended
presentation logic.

3
00:00:09.661 --> 00:00:20.274
Data may come from users, databases, uploaded files, third-party APIs,
logs, diagnostics, administrative fields, translation files, markdown,
rich text, or service integrations.

4
00:00:20.274 --> 00:00:26.125
Some sources feel internal, but that does not automatically make them safe
for every browser context.

5
00:00:26.125 --> 00:00:29.798
Data often flows from request to storage to template to browser.

6
00:00:29.798 --> 00:00:39.459
A user enters a profile description, a support team updates an
administrative note, a partner API sends a display name, or a log entry is
shown in an operations dashboard.

7
00:00:39.459 --> 00:00:51.297
The original input path may validate length or format, but the later
rendering path decides whether the value is safe in HTML, an attribute, a
URL, a script-adjacent structure, an email, or a document export.

8
00:00:51.297 --> 00:00:56.943
Stored content can become risky later when it is rendered in a new context
or shown to a new audience.

9
00:00:56.943 --> 00:01:00.889
A field created for plain text may later be reused inside a link title.

10
00:01:00.889 --> 00:01:03.474
A markdown field may later appear in an email.

11
00:01:03.474 --> 00:01:06.944
A diagnostic message may later be included in an admin table.

12
00:01:06.944 --> 00:01:14.155
Secure rendering requires teams to track not only what data is accepted,
but also where it will be displayed and who will see it.

13
00:01:14.155 --> 00:01:19.802
Developers should document sensitive rendering paths the same way they
document sensitive APIs.

14
00:01:19.802 --> 00:01:22.592
Which templates display user-controlled data?

15
00:01:22.592 --> 00:01:25.041
Which pages are viewed by administrators?

16
00:01:25.041 --> 00:01:27.082
Which fields support rich content?

17
00:01:27.082 --> 00:01:28.782
Which helpers return markup?

18
00:01:28.782 --> 00:01:31.232
Which exports or emails reuse web content?

19
00:01:31.232 --> 00:01:40.280
These questions make trust boundaries visible and help reviewers find
places where data may have crossed from safe value into unsafe
presentation behavior.
